// AN ENUM THAT EMBEDS ANOTHER ENUM.
//
// # THE THREE SPELLINGS, MEASURED IN BOTH ENGINES RATHER THAN ARGUED
//
// The slice that admitted this shape first predicted it was DECLARATION-ONLY —
// that no program could build a value — on the strength of two `nomi run`
// transcripts. That was wrong, and the correction is what this fixture pins:
//
//	Drawable.Shape{}                   CONSTRUCTS. interpreter "shape", and the
//	                                   VM agrees byte for byte
//	Drawable.Shape(Shape.Circle(1.5))  the front end ACCEPTS it, the interpreter
//	                                   TRAPS at run time ("cannot call Shape"),
//	                                   and this builder refuses by name
//	                                   (`variant construction`)
//	f(Shape.Circle(1.5))               a front-end error: an embedded ENUM does
//	                                   not widen the way an embedded STRUCT does
//	                                   ("argument 1: expected Drawable, got Shape")
//
// The first row is the one that matters and it is the one the wrong prediction
// missed: the value IS reachable, so admitting the declaration is not a bet on
// an empty population. It had to be checked rather than reasoned about, because
// "the interpreter rejected the two spellings I tried" and "no spelling works"
// are different claims and only the second licenses the admission.
//
// The `Drawable.Point` arm is the CONTROL. Without it this fixture would show
// an enum-embedding declaration lowering and say nothing about whether the
// struct-embedding half — the half that WIDENS — still works.
import std/io

enum Shape {
    Circle Float
    Square Int
}

struct Point {
    x: Int
    y: Int
}

enum Drawable {
    embeds Point
    embeds Shape
}

fn describe(d: Drawable): String {
    case d {
        Drawable.Point{x: px, y: _} -> "point " + Int.to_string(px)
        Drawable.Shape(s) -> "shape"
    }
}

fn main() {
    io.print(describe(Drawable.Shape{}))
    io.print(describe(Point{x: 3, y: 4}))
}
